---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { lanes } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Method"
  description="How worldviews are compared: the steelman rule, the comparison structure, and the standpoint of the site."
>
  <div class="container page">
    <h1>Method</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        The site asks which worldview gives the most coherent account of reality as
        actually encountered: existence, knowledge, logic, personality, morality, beauty,
        evil, guilt, suffering, death, history, and human destiny.
      </p>

      <h2>Standpoint</h2>
      <p>
        The site is written from a confessional Reformed Christian standpoint. It does
        not claim a neutral position above all worldviews. At the same time, every
        competing worldview must be presented in a form that a serious adherent or
        scholar could recognize as fair.
      </p>

      <h2>The six comparison lanes</h2>
      <ol>
        {lanes.map((name) => <li>{name}</li>)}
      </ol>
      <p>
        These are comparison families, not claims of total internal uniformity. Internal
        diversity is surfaced whenever it materially changes an answer.
      </p>

      <h2>The steelman rule</h2>
      <p>Before any Christian critique is written, the entry must answer:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>What does this worldview actually claim?</li>
        <li>Which major school or thinker is being represented?</li>
        <li>What explanatory work does the view genuinely perform well?</li>
        <li>Are important internal alternatives being omitted?</li>
        <li>Can the steelman be supported from a primary source or serious internal authority?</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How entries are structured</h2>
      <p>
        Entries for non-Christian worldviews present the <strong>View</strong>,
        <strong>What it explains well</strong>, the <strong>Christian response</strong>,
        and a <strong>Pressure point</strong>. Entries for Christianity present the
        <strong>Christian view</strong>, <strong>What it explains well</strong>, the
        <strong>Strongest objection</strong>, and the <strong>Christian reply</strong>.
        Christianity is tested too.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Worldviews are sourced first from primary texts, then from major thinkers within
        the tradition, then from high-quality academic scholarship for historical and
        contextual claims. A hostile secondary source is never the sole authority for
        what another tradition teaches.
      </p>

      <h2>Tone</h2>
      <p>
        Precise and non-mocking, with no triumphalist scoring and no numeric worldview
        scores. The site distinguishes what another worldview says, what historians and
        textual scholars establish, and what the Christian concludes from those facts.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
